<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tieba Traoré</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tieba Traore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tieba Traoré (died 1893) was king of Kénédougou in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>, who turned the loose chiefdom into a strong state and moved its capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikasso">Sikasso</a>, which he fortified with great earthen walls. He withstood a long siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samori-ture">Samori Ture</a> in 1887–1888 and allied with the French against him. His brother Ba Bemba succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tieba-traore.jpg" alt="Tieba Traoré" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KAG1LP2MDIAKITE · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ECOLE_CAMP_TIEBA_SIKASSO_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
